 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Probably not just you, but Perth's price rise is smaller than the national one.** The CPI data suggests that if your bill has jumped, it's more likely from your usage or a rebate change than from a big rise in Perth electricity prices.\n\n- **Perth electricity prices, August 2026:** up 2.4% on August 2025. The index is 102.4 against 100 a year earlier.\n- **Australia-wide, August 2026:** up 13.2% on a year earlier.\n- **Perth's path:** prices fell about 29% from mid-2025 to November 2025 (index 69.2), then rose back to 100 by February 2026. The swings look driven by state and federal government energy rebates starting and stopping, so year-on-year comparisons are noisy.\n\nThe CPI tracks average prices, not your bill. A few things could explain a high bill:\n\n1. **Usage:** a hot summer, air conditioning, pool pumps, or an electric hot water system can roughly double a bill. Compare your kWh for this quarter with the same quarter last year.\n2. **Rebates:** check whether a WA energy rebate credit appeared on last year's bill but not this one. Its timing has changed, so a missing credit can make one bill look much bigger.\n3. **Tariff or supply charges:** check your per-kWh rate and daily supply charge against your last bill. Your retailer or the Synergy/Western Power tariff schedule will show any change.\n4. **Estimated reads:** if the bill is based on an estimate, a later actual reading can produce a large catch-up charge.\n\nIf you send me the kWh used and the total from your last two bills, I can help work out whether it's usage or price.",
